adj:note-oracle-term
    a vfr:DesignNote ;
    vfr:order 18 ;
    vfr:entryTitle "design note: the oracle term, defined" ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo adj:mzargham ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    vfr:noteText """i think we may need to provide a definition for oracle or swap in a term from travis' paper that suffices for process than answers a question. in my work i would consider it an authoritative source of truth but he does not use that term""" ;
    vfr:changeNote """the term is kept and defined rather than swapped: the paper has no term for the provider of a value a gate reads, so the walkthrough now defines oracle at first use and in the model's OracleService doc — an oracle is a process that answers a question: an identified service the Op queries for a value it does not compute itself, with each exchange cited (service, request payload, response code, response). The definition is marked as the adjudicator's term, introduced by the GAP-05 ruling; the adjudicator's own preferred term from outside this engagement is deliberately not used.""" .

vfr:Oracle
    rdfs:label "Oracle" ;
    rdfs:comment "A process that answers a question: an identified service the Op queries for a value it does not compute itself, with each exchange cited (service, request payload, response code, response payload). The adjudicator's term, introduced by the GAP-05 ruling; the paper does not use it. The usage follows the oracle machine of computability theory (Turing 1939, Systems of Logic Based on Ordinals, doi:10.1112/plms/s2-45.1.161): a machine that consults an external source for answers it does not compute." ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oracle_machine> ;
    vfr:introducedBy adj:ruling-gap05 .

